859d0cef4c206e291d31eba2a980732b497af31.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/example-7163.json" "file_sha256" "f0f4c612ae4034cf6f2448643db2dea6733e95326e177702c97046f2d276c51d"} {"question" "e-7163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overline B,$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $B.$ To represent $\\overline B,$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $B,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/assets/daad235ef63e127e99bf3eec7b7bbe75fe6adb969f5b54ebb5a3fb010773b680.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $a,$ $c,$ $e,$ and $g.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $B.$ Therefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{B} = \\{ a,c,e,g \\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/example-7163.json" "file_sha256" "f0f4c612ae4034cf6f2448643db2dea6733e95326e177702c97046f2d276c51d"} {"question" "e-7164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U = \\{ a, b, c, d, e, f, g \\}$ be the universal set and let $A = \\{ b, c, d, f, g \\}.$ What is $\\overline{A}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/example-7164.json" "file_sha256" "71e02415b594bec449f894dc35c3d17b341b8542d92b1f58628090cb3d47ff0a"} {"question" "e-7164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The complement of $A,$ denoted $\\overline{A},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A.$\n\nThe only elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are $a$ and $e.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{A} = \\{ a, e \\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/example-7164.json" "file_sha256" "71e02415b594bec449f894dc35c3d17b341b8542d92b1f58628090cb3d47ff0a"} {"question" "e-7165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U = \\{ 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 30 \\}$ be the universal set and let $B = \\{ 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 28 \\}.$ What is $\\overline{B}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/example-7165.json" "file_sha256" "f352a17f38a1f36cc6465b564ba909f15685dd4a9c5892120fd770d57e94bc8f"} {"question" "e-7165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overline{B},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the integers $1$ through $30,$ while the set $B$ consists of the integers $5$ through $28.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ $4,$ $29,$ and $30.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{B} = \\{ 1,2,3,4,29,30 \\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/example-7165.json" "file_sha256" "f352a17f38a1f36cc6465b564ba909f15685dd4a9c5892120fd770d57e94bc8f"}] "usage" {"e-7165" [] "e-7163" [] "e-7164" [] "q-1236" []} "basis" 1255 "retractions" [[17592186157502 :question/problem "If $U = {t, u, v, w, x, y}$ is the universal set and $S = {w, x, y}$, then the complement of $S$ is"] [17592186157023 :question/problem "Let $U = \\{a, b, c, d, e, f, g \\}$ be the universal set and let $B = \\{b, d, f \\}$. What is $\\overline{B}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2829/Source/Images/e3286d4e435a020e578a009552a1be73.png)"] [17592186157023 :question/worked-solution "The complement of $B$, denoted $\\overline B$, consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $B$. To represent $\\overline B$, we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $B$, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2829/Source/Images/9385e4e8c3b0eab7bcb5e0f0d1bb2955.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $a$, $c$, $e$, and $g$. These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $B$. Therefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{B} = \\{a,c,e,g \\}\n$$"] [17592186157024 :question/problem "Let $U = \\{a, b, c, d, e, f, g \\}$ be the universal set and let $A = \\{b, c, d, f, g \\}$. What is $\\overline{A}$?"] [17592186157024 :question/worked-solution "The complement of $A$, denoted $\\overline{A}$, consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A$.\n\nThe only elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are $a$ and $e$.\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{A} = \\{a, e \\}\n$$"] [17592186157025 :question/problem "Let $U = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 30 \\}$ be the universal set and let $B = \\{5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 28 \\}$. What is $\\overline{B}$?"] [17592186157025 :question/worked-solution "The complement of $B$, denoted $\\overline{B}$, consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B$.\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the integers $1$ through $30$, while the set $B$ consists of the integers $5$ through $28$.\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $1$, $2$, $3$, $4$, $29$, and $30$.\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{B} = \\{1,2,3,4,29,30 \\}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186153588 17592186153589 17592186153590 17592186153591 17592186153592] "immutable_fields" [[17592186153587 :answer-field/correct] [17592186153587 :answer-field/id] [17592186153587 :answer-field/key] [17592186153587 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
